 Y is  different from the predicted label f of  x  in practice however this classification  error is usually not our only  consideration  for example if we want to deploy a model  to make decisions about people then  fairness is another important  consideration  for two models with the same  classification error it could be the  case that one model is much better than  the other in terms of fairness  considerations  also classification error only counts  the number of mistakes our model is  making but it doesn't capture how many  but it doesn't capture which mistakes  our model is making  and in applications such as self-driving  cars it is important to avoid serious  mistakes when we cannot avoid all  mistakes  accuracy in multi-calibration can be  viewed as learning Frameworks that allow  us to capture these various goals that  appear in practice and in fact these  Notions allow us to customize our  learning goal  more specifically multi accuracy and  multi-calibration are in this General  framework of learning real valued  hypothesis here we focus on the  realizable settings so there is a source  hypothesis class and every Source  hypothesis in this class is a real  valued function that Maps individuals to  real numbers between -1 and 1.  we assume that our data is labeled  according to a source hypothesis and  what this means in this real valued  setting is that the conditional  expectation of the label y given the  individual X is equal to S of x  given data our learner outputs a Model F  which is also a real valued function  which Maps individuals to real numbers  between -1 and 1.  the goal is to achieve them either the  multi-accuracy guarantee or the  multi-calibration guarantee with  probability at least Y minus Delta  if you're not familiar with these  Notions these equations might look  complicated to you but what's important  here is that both multi-accuracy and  multi-calibration are defined with  respect to another hypothesis class the  distinguisher class here the  distinguisher class consists of  distinguishers which are again function  real valued functions mapping  individuals to real numbers between -1  and 1.  the power of multi accuracy and  multi-calibration is that they allow us  to customize our learning goal by  choosing the distinguisher class D  and this also makes multi accuracy in  multi-calibration become learning tasks  involving a pair of hypothesis classes  here we have the source hypothesis class  s and the distinguisher class D  in this paper we show that the sample  complexity of distribution free realized  about multi-accuracy and  multi-calibration is characterized by  the mutual fat shattering dimension of  the two hypothesis classes s and D  as we mentioned earlier this result  solves an open problem in our earlier  paper with Omar rangos and we proved  this result as an application of our  sample complexity characterization for  comparative learning  here I would like to verify that  even when theorem Y is given are proof  of theorem 2 is still non-trivial and it  requires a lot of work  now I would like to talk about how we  prove theorem 1. as we you will see our  proof is pretty short but it is very  interesting and cute proof and it makes  an insightful connection to learning  partial hypothesis  the task of learning partial hypothesis  has been started in several previous  papers and it has a realizable setting  and an agnostic setting here I will  focus on the agnostic setting due to  time constraint  as usual in the agnostic setting we make  no assumption on the data distribution  and how the goal is to Output the model  with the classification error are  propounded by the best classification  error achievable by a hypothesis in a  hypothesis class plus some small Epsilon  the only difference is that the compart  the hypothesis we consider here may be  partial in the sense that they map some  individuals to plus one minus one but  they can also map some in some  individuals to the undefined label which  is represented by the star here  for these partial hypothesis the  classification error is still defined as  the probability that the true label Y is  different from the predicted label h of  X and since H may be partial in the case  where H of X is star we always have y  being different from h of X this is  because the true label Y is always  either plus 1 or -1  and it never takes the value star  in this partial hypothesis learning  setting  in a previous paper by Alan Hanukkah  hoseman and Martin  the authors showed that the simple  complexity of reliably agnostically  learning partial hypothesis is  characterized by the VC dimension of the  partial hypothesis class  for a partial hypothesis crossed the VC  Dimension is defined as usual to be the  maximum size of a shattered subset and  here we only consider shattering using  plus ones and minus ones  our approach for proving our main result  theorem 1 is by reduction to this  previous result theorem 3. and  specifically for a comparative learning  task with the source class S and A  Benchmark Class B we want to construct a  partial hypothesis class H so that we  can relate the partial so that we can  relate the comparative learning task for  S and B to the task of learning the  partial hypothesis class h  the way we construct this partial  hypothesis class H is as follows we take  every pair of source hypothesis s and  Benchmark hypothesis B and we construct  an agreement hypothesis a sub s b  if SNP assigned the same label to an  individual then the agreement hypothesis  also assigns the same label to that  individual as S and P  if SNB assign different labels to an  individual then the agreement hypothesis  assigns the undefined label star to that  individual  we construct the partial hypothesis  class H so that it consists of all such  agreement hypothesis for every pair of S  and B  coming from the source class and The  Benchmark class with respectively  our construction of the partial  hypothesis cost H allows us to focus on  the complexity shared by the two classes  S and B and ignore the complexity that  is only present in one of the classes  using the partial hypothesis class H we  can prove these three lemmas which shows  that the VC dimension of the partial  hypothesis class H is equal to the  mutual BC dimension of the classes S and  B and we can relate the comparative  learning task for S and B to the task of  learning the partial hypothesis class h  using these lemmas we are able to prove  our main result theorem 1 using this  previous result theorem 3. and our proof  for theorem of 1 also works when S and B  themselves are partial hypothesis  classes  finally here is a list of takeaways  thank you for watching
 

